1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,410
Section 3

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:03,840
Ruth:

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:08,130
Ed, how are you getting on with the reading for our presentation next week?

4
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:08,312
Ed:

5
00:00:08,312 --> 00:00:09,886
Well, okay Ruth,

6
00:00:09,886 --> 00:00:11,440
but there's so much of it.

7
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:12,065
Ruth:

8
00:00:12,065 --> 00:00:12,226
I know.

9
00:00:12,226 --> 00:00:16,960
I hadn't realized birth order was such a popular area of research.

10
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:17,010
Ed:

11
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:21,300
But the stuff on birth order and personality is mostly unreliable.

12
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:26,412
From what I've been reading, a lot of the claims about how you position

13
00:00:26,412 --> 00:00:31,525
in the family determines certain personality traits are just stereotypes, with no robust evidence

14
00:00:31,525 --> 00:00:32,620
to support them.

15
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:32,670
Ruth:

16
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:33,483
Okay,

17
00:00:33,483 --> 00:00:35,640
but that's an interesting point.

18
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:39,830
We could start by outlining what previous research has shown.

19
00:00:39,830 --> 00:00:42,730
There are studies going back over 100 years.

20
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:42,780
Ed:

21
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:43,421
Yeah,

22
00:00:43,421 --> 00:00:48,256
so we could just run through some of the typical traits, like the consensus

23
00:00:48,256 --> 00:00:51,711
seems to be that oldest children are generally less well-adjusted

24
00:00:51,711 --> 00:00:55,510
because they never get over the arrival of a younger sibling.

25
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:55,560
Ruth:

26
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:56,129
Right,

27
00:00:56,129 --> 00:01:00,462
but on a positive note, some studies claim that they were thought to be

28
00:01:00,462 --> 00:01:01,390
good at nurturing.

29
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:02,672
Certainly in the past,

30
00:01:02,672 --> 00:01:07,159
when people had large families, they would have been expected to look after the

31
00:01:07,159 --> 00:01:07,800
younger ones.

32
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:07,850
Ed:

33
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:10,768
There isn't such a clear picture for middle children,

34
00:01:10,768 --> 00:01:14,922
but one trait that a lot of the studies mention is that they are

35
00:01:14,922 --> 00:01:17,890
easier to get on with an older or younger siblings.

36
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:17,940
Ruth:

37
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:23,555
Mm, generally eager to please and helpful, although that certainly not accurate as far

38
00:01:23,555 --> 00:01:28,464
as my family goes, my middle brother was a nightmare, always causing fights

39
00:01:28,464 --> 00:01:30,730
and envious of whatever I had.

40
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:30,780
Ed:

41
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:34,610
As I said, none of these seems to relate to my own experience.

42
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:38,633
I'm the youngest in my family and I don't recognize myself in any of

43
00:01:38,633 --> 00:01:40,070
the studies I've read about.

44
00:01:40,070 --> 00:01:45,957
I'm supposed to have been a sociable and confident child who made friends easily,

45
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:48,480
but I was actually terribly shy.

46
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:48,530
Ruth:

47
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:49,535
Really?

48
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:50,590
that's funny.

49
00:01:50,590 --> 00:01:53,664
There have been hundreds of studies on twins,

50
00:01:53,664 --> 00:01:55,970
but mostly about nurture versus nature.

51
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:56,312
Ed:

52
00:01:56,312 --> 00:02:01,093
There was one on personality which said that a twin is likely to be

53
00:02:01,093 --> 00:02:02,801
quite shy in social situations,

54
00:02:02,801 --> 00:02:06,900
because they always have their twin around to depend on for support.

55
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:07,193
Ruth:

56
00:02:07,193 --> 00:02:08,661
My cousins were like that.

57
00:02:08,661 --> 00:02:11,889
When they were small, they were only interested in each other

58
00:02:11,889 --> 00:02:14,530
and found it hard to engage with other kids.

59
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:16,070
They're fine now though.

60
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:16,468
Ed:

61
00:02:16,468 --> 00:02:19,650
Only children have had a really bad press.

62
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:24,444
A lot of studies of branded them as loners who think the world revolves around them

63
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:27,440
because they've never had to fight for their parents' attention.

64
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:27,771
Ruth:

65
00:02:27,771 --> 00:02:29,760
That does seem a bit harsh.

66
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:34,840
One category I hadn't considered before was children with much older siblings.

67
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:39,000
A couple of studies mention that these children grow up more quickly

68
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,160
and are expected to do basic things for themselves like getting dressed.

69
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:43,543
Ed:

70
00:02:43,543 --> 00:02:48,912
I can see how that might be true, although I expect that sometimes the

71
00:02:48,912 --> 00:02:53,130
exact opposite, playing the baby role, from clamoring for special treatment.

72
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:53,401
Ruth:

73
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:56,650
What was the problem with most of these studies, do you think?

74
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:57,016
Ed:

75
00:02:57,016 --> 00:03:02,138
I think it was because in a lot of cases, data was collected from

76
00:03:02,138 --> 00:03:03,968
only one sibling per family,

77
00:03:03,968 --> 00:03:09,090
who rated him or herself and his or her siblings at the same time.

78
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:09,484
Ruth:

79
00:03:09,484 --> 00:03:14,217
Mm, some of the old research into the relationship between birth order

80
00:03:14,217 --> 00:03:18,160
and academic achievement has been proved to be accurate though.

81
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:23,665
Performances in intelligence tests declined slightly from the eldest child to his

82
00:03:23,665 --> 00:03:25,500
or her younger siblings.

83
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,390
This has been proved in lots of recent studies.

84
00:03:28,390 --> 00:03:28,781
Ed:

85
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:33,860
Yes, although what many of them didn't take into consideration was family size.

86
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:39,155
The more siblings that are the likely of the family is to have a

87
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:40,290
low socioeconomic status,

88
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:44,450
which can also account for differences between siblings in academic performance.

89
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:44,757
Ruth:

90
00:03:44,757 --> 00:03:49,050
The oldest boy might be given more opportunities than his younger sisters, for example.

91
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:49,535
Ed:

92
00:03:49,535 --> 00:03:50,020
Exactly.

93
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:50,414
Ruth:

94
00:03:50,414 --> 00:03:55,933
But the main reason for the marginally higher academic performance of oldest children is

95
00:03:55,933 --> 00:03:57,510
quite surprising, I think.

96
00:03:57,510 --> 00:04:02,503
It's not only that they benefit intellectually from extra attention at a young age,

97
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:05,000
which is what I would have expected.

98
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,530
It's that they benefit from being teachers for their younger siblings by verbalizing processes.

99
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:10,964
Ed:

100
00:04:10,964 --> 00:04:11,397
Right.

101
00:04:11,397 --> 00:04:14,433
And this gives them status and confidence,

102
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:18,770
which again contributes in a small way to better performance.

103
00:04:18,770 --> 00:04:23,570
So would you say sibling rivalry has been a useful thing for you.

104
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:23,915
Ruth:

105
00:04:23,915 --> 00:04:24,950
I think so.

106
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:28,590
My younger brother was incredibly annoying and we fought a lot,

107
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:31,900
but I think this has made me a stronger person.

108
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,140
I know how to defend myself.

109
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:38,538
We had some terrible arguments and I would have died rather than apologize to

110
00:04:38,538 --> 00:04:38,852
him,

111
00:04:38,852 --> 00:04:41,680
but we had to put up with each other.

112
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,210
And most of the time we coexisted amicably enough.

113
00:04:45,210 --> 00:04:45,614
Ed:

114
00:04:45,614 --> 00:04:48,034
Yes, my situation was pretty similar,

115
00:04:48,034 --> 00:04:53,280
but I don't think having two older brothers made me any less selfish.

116
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:56,750
I was never prepared to let my brothers use any of my stuff.

117
00:04:56,750 --> 00:04:57,111
Ed:

118
00:04:57,111 --> 00:04:59,278
That's perfectly normal, whereas some time...

119
00:04:59,278 --> 00:05:00,000
(fade out)
